00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:07.670 align:start position:0%
rail safety is a top priority as eu  transport ministers meet in luxembourg  on october 10.  the ministers want to agree on a common  approach toward new  safety rules he aims to help create  economies of scale on rail projects  across europe  and simplify the procedures for issuing  safety certificates  current rules date from 2004 the plan is  to move toward a single eu safety  certificate  and do away with the need for national  rules the roles of national rail  authorities  and the european railway agency are to  be reviewed too  airline passenger rights and europe's  air traffic control system are also high  on the agenda  the ministers will debate the kinds of  protection that might be offered to  passengers who miss flight connections  while not discouraging air carriers and  tour organizers  from offering a wide choice of routes  and ticket combinations  on top of that they'll look at how to  regulate compensation for long flight  delays  council is also expected to decide  whether to extend a key project on  european air traffic management research  by  eight years until the end of 2024.  maritime transport is also up for  discussion notably pollution at sea  the ministers want to reach a common  position on a proposal for financing the  activities of the european  maritime safety agency with a focus on  combating marine pollution  for the next seven years the agency has  been in operation for a decade  the budgetary ceiling for its  anti-pollution activities until 2020  could reach just over 160 million euros
 

